a stub answers with the right arity, in the right register, and a duration is text

Every generated stub here declared its methods v@:, meaning no arguments and no return. That
is wrong twice over and both were fatal:

  the ARITY. A caller passing arguments writes through slots the invocation does not have:
      NSForwardSignatureError: invoked with 4 args, but 2 expected. Selector addView:inGravity:
  and the run ended in SIGSEGV inside objc_retain on a value that was never an object.

  the RETURN. Nothing is written to the return register, so an initialiser hands back
  whatever was in it. iA Writer sent a message to 0x4e and died in objc_msgSend.

The selector carries the arity: one argument per colon. Assuming every argument is an object
is wrong for a primitive, but it keeps the frame the right SIZE, which is the difference
between a wrong value and a crash. forwardInvocation now sets a nil return.

25 classes here and 81 more in cocotron, all from the same template. NSForwardSignatureError
went from dozens in a run to one.

NSDateComponentsFormatter gets a real answer as well, because nil was its whole behaviour and
a caller that copied the result died in CFStringCreateCopy on a null string. It renders the
components it is given in the plain 1h 2m 3s shape; it does not consult allowedUnits, and what
it guarantees is a string.

